 ],
 "literal": "Bent bow-string shining, strength-filled strong leg, / hard-to-torment fierce strength, my lord, stretching after bending, / like a tiger abiding in a cave coming for prey, / not respecting his hard-to-oppose chest, roaring and rising, / \"We are noble, we are great, than us / the warrior is young, and the plunder is great,\" saying, / the new warriors who came with scorn, / with dim eyes, to retreat on their backs, / here he would not agree to kill them, but there, their / women wearing glorious jewels, filled with shame, to perish, / in the very place of their fathers' town, / going while the clear kiṇai drum resounded, there he killed them indeed.",
 "translation": "With a bent bow-string shining on his strong, powerful legs, my lord of fierce strength, hard to torment, stretched himself like a cave-dwelling tiger emerging for its prey. Disregarding his chest, which is hard to oppose, the newly arrived warriors rose up roaring, saying, 'We are noble, we are great! The king who opposes us is young, and the plunder we can take is vast.' But as those scornful warriors retreated with dim, lusterless eyes, my lord did not deign to slay them here on the battlefield. Instead, he went to their fathers' own towns, and there, as the clear-sounding kiṇai drums resounded, he slew them, causing their beautifully jeweled women to perish in shame.",
 "modern": "வளைந்த வில் நாணுடன் விளங்கும் வலிமை மிக்க கால்களையும், வெல்ல முடியாத கடுமையான ஆற்றலையும் உடைய என் தலைவன், குகையில் வாழும் புலி இரைக்காக வெளியே வந்து உடலை நிமிர்த்துவது போல நின்றான். அவனது எதிர்க்க முடியாத மார்பை மதிக்காமல், 'நாம் சிறந்தவர், பெரியவர்; நம்மை எதிர்க்கும் அரசனோ இளைஞன், கொள்ளையிடக்கூடிய பொருளும் மிக அதிகம்' என்று இகழ்ந்து கூறி ஆரவாரத்துடன் வந்த புதிய வீரர்கள், ஒளி இழந்த கண்களுடன் போர்க்களத்திலிருந்து பின்வாங்கி ஓடினர். அவர்களை இங்கேயே கொல்வதற்கு என் தலைவன் விரும்பவில்லை. மாறாக, அவர்களின் தந்தையரின் ஊர்களுக்கே சென்று, தெளிந்த ஓசையுடைய கிணைப் பறைகள் முழங்க, அங்கேயே அவர்களைக் கொன்று வீழ்த்தினான். அதனால், சிறந்த அணிகலன்களை அணிந்த அவர்களின் பெண்கள் நாணமடைந்து அழியும்படி செய்தான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Puṟam genre, specifically celebrating the military valor of a young king. The poet, Iṭaikkuṉṟūr Kiḻār, praises the king's fierce strength by comparing him to a cave-dwelling tiger that stretches itself before pouncing on its prey. The enemy kings had underestimated him because of his youth, arrogantly assuming that his kingdom would be easy to plunder. However, when they faced him, they were utterly defeated and forced to retreat in shame. Rather than slaying them on the battlefield, the king pursued them to their own ancestral towns to destroy them completely, leaving their noble women to perish in grief and shame. Key words in the poem include 'uḻuvai' (tiger), 'iḷaiyaṉ' (young), and 'teṇkiṇai' (clear-sounding drum).",
